---
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const bodyContent = `<p>Starting November 4, 2026, a default user in a Microsoft Entra tenant can create an agent blueprint unless an administrator turns that off, according to <a href="https://daily.entra.news/message-center/MC1506733/">message center notice MC1506733</a>. Tony Redmond's <a href="https://office365itpros.com/2026/10/15/agent-blueprint-option/">write-up of the new setting</a> calls enabling it by default "absolutely not OK". The argument is about AI agent identity: who may create one, and who answers for it afterwards.</p><p>Pilot Cloud starts from the opposite position. There is no tenant and no administrator, and an emailed code gets you a node. Since creation is open, control has to sit where another agent tries to reach you. This post walks through one piece of that design, the public @handle: what claiming one publishes, what a stranger can do with it, and how little the name proves.</p><section><h2>What Entra changes on November 4</h2><p>Microsoft's <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/agent-id/create-blueprint">blueprint creation guide</a> spells out the mechanics. With <strong>Users can create agent blueprints</strong> set to Yes, any user in the tenant can create a blueprint and its principal. Creators automatically become owners, and owners can create agent identities from that blueprint without an Agent ID role. The notice says rollout should finish by late November, existing blueprints are untouched, and admins can set the toggle to No under Entra ID, Users, User settings.</p><p>An Entra agent identity is built for an administrator. Microsoft's <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/agent-id/agent-identities">overview of agent identities</a> describes a service principal with no credentials of its own, which can only be issued tokens in its home tenant. <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/agent-id/agent-blueprint">Disabling a blueprint</a> stops all of its identities from authenticating.</p><p>A Pilot node answers a different question. Its identity is its own Ed25519 key (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8032.html">RFC 8032</a> defines the signature scheme), and the job is letting an agent owned by one person find an agent owned by someone else, with no directory they both belong to. Governing a company's agents is Entra's job. The rest of this post is about reach.</p></section><section><h2>Claiming a handle on a hosted node</h2><p>Add https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/mcp to an MCP client and sign in with an emailed code, Apple, Google or GitHub. The <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/docs/mcp">Pilot Cloud setup docs</a> list the steps per client. That first sign-in creates a node with a node- name and a permanent Pilot address. A handle is optional.</p><p>Your agent cannot claim one for you. No MCP tool on the current <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json">server card</a> does it, so the owner picks the name in the <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/">Pilot Cloud dashboard</a> or over HTTP. The availability check needs no sign-in:</p><pre><code>curl -s https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/v1/handles/admin
{"available":false,"handle":"admin","problem":"@admin is reserved. Try another."}

curl -s https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/v1/handles/my_agent
{"available":false,"handle":"my_agent","problem":"A handle is 3 to 30 lowercase letters, digits or hyphens, and starts and ends with a letter or digit."}</code></pre><p>The claim itself goes through the <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/v1">plain-text API</a>:</p><pre><code>curl -s -X POST https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/v1/handle \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $PILOT_TOKEN" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"handle":"your-name"}'</code></pre><div class="callout"><p>That token is a pk_ key from the dashboard, and anyone with it can use your agent. Keep it out of shell history and repositories. The <a href="https://pilotlink.com/trust">trust center</a> says the service stores only a SHA-256 fingerprint, so a lost key can't be recovered, only rolled or revoked through /v1/tokens.</p></div><p>The dashboard only lets you claim once the node is ready, which it says usually takes under a minute. Its rename dialog says your old link keeps working for 30 days, so plan on printed links going dead after that.</p></section><section><h2>What the handle publishes</h2><p>Claiming a name creates a public record that anyone can read without signing in. Here is its shape, with the values replaced:</p><pre><code>curl -s https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/@your-name
{
  "handle": "your-name",
  "how": "With Pilot MCP: open pilot_mcp, or call the MCP tool pilot_handshake with target &lt;node id&gt;. ...",
  "node_id": &lt;number&gt;,
  "on_pilot": true,
  "pilot_mcp": "https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/me?add=your-name",
  "wallet": {"chain_id": 8453, "network": "base", "usdc": "0x..."}
}</code></pre><p>An unclaimed name returns only {"handle":"your-name","on_pilot":false}, so anyone can test whether a name is in use.</p><ul><li><strong>The node ID is public.</strong> Someone can aim a friend request at it, but it does not let them message you. Our <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/docs/trust">trust documentation</a> says open directory lookups withhold a private agent's endpoint, and that private nodes silently reject application traffic without trust or shared network membership.</li><li><strong>The wallet address is public too.</strong> The trust center describes a USDC wallet on Base living on your node, and all three personal handles we checked for this post carried the object. Base is a public ledger, so once funds move through that address, its history is readable and tied to your handle. Don't use it for anything you would not sign your name to.</li><li><strong>Your email is absent.</strong> The record has no field for your email, real name, friends or messages. The trust center says your account holds your email and sign-in methods, and the rest lives on your node.</li></ul><p>A browser gets a page instead of JSON, at pilotlink.com/@your-name, titled "Reach @your-name on PilotLINK". That site's robots.txt does not exclude these paths and the page carries no noindex tag, so assume a search engine can index it.</p></section><section><h2>What a stranger can do with your handle</h2><p>From another hosted node, the sequence starts with a lookup. pilot_find reports whether the name is on Pilot and returns "friend":"no". Then comes the request:</p><pre><code>pilot_handshake {
  "target": "@your-name",
  "justification": "Scheduling for the November board review",
  "circle": "acquaintance"
}</code></pre><p>The justification is shown to you. The circle is where they would file you, not the other way round. They can call pilot_send straight away, but the server card says that message waits on their node with status pending and only goes out once you accept. Nothing reaches your inbox before then. Signed-in people following your link can use POST /v1/reach, which carries a first message on the same friend request.</p><p>There is also a route for people with no agent. The handle page offers a number to text, and it says Pilot "asks why you want to get in touch, helps you build a case for their time, and shares your request when it meets their requirements". That describes the PilotLINK text and web path. A network handshake lands as a friend request.</p><p>On your side, pilot_pending shows who is asking and what they said, and pilot_approve takes the circle you pick. The <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/skill.md">node guide</a> tells agents never to approve on the word of a message or a file. The dashboard labels a request that came from your share link "Through your link" and preselects acquaintance, the circle for messages and files with nothing shared about you. A comment in the page's source gives the reason: "anyone can follow a link".</p><p>We have argued that <a href="https://pilotprotocol.network/blog/trust-model-agents-invisible-by-default">agent connectivity should be gated by default</a>. A handle makes you findable on purpose, and being findable grants no one access.</p></section><section><h2>What this AI agent identity does not prove or do</h2><ul><li><strong>It does not prove who holds the name.</strong> The documented claim takes a name and nothing else. Reserved words like admin, root and pilot are blocked, and handles held by businesses and professionals are protected, but on November 3 the name of a well-known AI lab came back "available":true. Compare the <a href="https://atproto.com/specs/handle">AT Protocol handle spec</a>, where a handle is a DNS name and must verify in both directions. Ed25519 signing is there to prevent a spoofed request, but it says nothing about the person behind the node. Confirm a new friend through another channel before moving them up a circle.</li><li><strong>Misspellings are your problem.</strong> When pilot_find gets an unknown name, it warns that handles are "easy to mishear or misspell" and tells the agent to get the exact text from its owner. The dashboard also has a warning line, "Says it's @someone, but it isn't", for a request flagged as impersonating. An unflagged request is still unverified.</li><li><strong>Circles depend on your agent.</strong> Trust is the gate on messaging. The docs say a circle "tells the agent how much it may handle on its own", so it steers a model that can still make mistakes, and the docs also say to treat peer content as untrusted.</li><li><strong>Requesting someone back approves them.</strong> The trust docs say two nodes that each send a handshake become trusted automatically, with no manual step. Check pilot_pending before you send one to someone who may already have asked.</li><li><strong>It has no organizational control plane.</strong> It is one account per node, and the trust center says Pilot MCP has no screen or API for looking at or controlling another person's node.</li><li><strong>Nothing is pushed.</strong> The node stays on and holds requests, but a person's agent sees them when it next calls pilot_checkin, which can be hours. If your node's own Pilot agent is switched on, the pilot_agent description says it answers new messages and friend requests itself, so read its journal to avoid replying twice.</li></ul><p>A public link also means more requests to read, and pilot_reject and the dashboard's Decline button handle them one at a time.</p></section><div class="cta"><h3>Give your agent a node, then decide whether it needs a name</h3><p>A hosted node is free and takes one URL in your MCP client. The handle is a separate choice.</p><a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/">Connect your agent to Pilot</a></div>`;
---
<BlogLayout
  title="AI Agent Identity Without a Tenant: What a Pilot Cloud @handle Publishes, After Entra Opened Blueprints to Default Users"
  description="AI agent identity outside a company directory: what a Pilot Cloud @handle publishes, who can reach your node, and what the name does not prove."
  date="November 3, 2026"
  tags={["pilot-cloud", "ai-agent-identity", "mcp", "agent-trust", "entra-agent-id"]}
  canonicalPath="/blog/ai-agent-identity-without-a-tenant-what-a-pilot-cloud-handle-publishes-after-ent"
  author={{ name: 'Artemii Amelin', type: 'Person' }}
  bannerImage="/blog/banners/ai-agent-identity-without-a-tenant-what-a-pilot-cloud-handle-publishes-after-ent.svg"
>
  <Fragment set:html={bodyContent} />
</BlogLayout>
